@role order
@guard needs: an external dated event (paper due, workshop, contract end, runway floor) and which candidates it bears on.
@effect raises candidates the date bears on and narrows their scope to what can be true by the date.
@predicts what is due on the date exists on the date.
@references [task-selection/end-in-something-shown task-selection/halfway-house]
! conclusion: An external date reorders the field and shrinks scopes to what can be made true by then.

  + context: An external event is dated, and some candidates feed it.

  + IF:
    A date is near enough that ordinary ordering would miss it.

  + HOWEVER:
    Deadline work displaces work with more leverage, and scope pressure invites claims that will not hold.

  + THEN:
    Raise what the date needs, cut scope to verification of what must be true by then, and record what was displaced. Most closure conditions have no deadline; do not invent one.

  + BECAUSE:
    A date is the one cost that rises with no action at all.

  + evidence:
    - futon2/holes/tickets/T-wm-excessive-guardrails-19092026.md:107-108: "undoing the layer costs more, against a 2-day deadline."
    - memory wm-closure-strategy-zai-owners (Joe 2026-09-16): "Paper due 2026-09-20; Joe withdraws it if the machine isn't done."
    - futon7/holes/M-futon-forward-model.md:319: "calendar pressure: runway depletes, OBU contract ends 2026-08-31 ... EFE shifts toward the hedge".
    - futon3c/holes/E-futon-workshop-demo.md:7-13: the workshop bounds scope to "assurance work"; feature work out of scope.

  + counter-evidence: futon7/holes/M-interim-director-proxy-metric-inventory.md:1306, "No closure-condition deadlines anywhere."
